{
  "name": "Santa Maria del Fiore -- Florence",
  "_about": "The cathedral at its real size: 153m from the facade to the back of the east tribune, a nave of four bays 20.3m each, an octagon 45.5m across the flats, and Brunelleschi's dome springing from the drum at 54.8m to an inner crown at 91m with the lantern over it. The nave arcade's arches are the widest of any Gothic church -- the four bays are as long as eight of Cologne's -- and that is the plan's own doing: Florence vaults a square-ish bay rather than a narrow one.\nTHE DOME is the engine's new `dome` part: a cloister vault over the octagon, eight webs meeting on eight groins, its meridian struck as the QUINTO ACUTO -- an arc at four fifths of the span, which is the pointed profile at spread 0.6, exactly how it was set out. Two shells, inner and outer, with the gap between them that the climbing stair runs in.\nTHE THREE TRIBUNES close the octagon's east, north and south sides: each a ring of five chapels on its own radius, placed and turned onto its own face of the octagon.\nTHE CAMPANILE stands clear of the church, as Giotto's does: 14.45m square, 84.7m tall, five storeys, with a flight of stairs in each.",
